{:edb/db-before-t 2433 :edb/db-after-t 2434 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535746 50 #inst "2026-10-09T06:28:02.621Z" 13194139535746 true] [17592186184092 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364571 13194139535746 true] [17592186184092 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364578 13194139535746 true] [17592186184093 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364585 13194139535746 true] [17592186184093 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364592 13194139535746 true] [17592186184788 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535746 true] [17592186184788 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069862/assets/a41a721eab2eb97e8b7b30ddf877e37a48d2b74eeef82ab0a4b72f3bb4a93603.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x+C),$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of $C?$" 13194139535746 true] [17592186184788 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3850/Source/Images/q-79194.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y = \\tan (2x + C)$, where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{π}{2} < C < \\frac{π}{2}$, what is the value of $C$?" 13194139535746 false] [17592186184788 1145 "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2(x+\\frac{C}{2}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{C}{2}$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2(x+\\frac{C}{2}))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=tan\\,(2x).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069862/assets/f08846ce1f79cd14c593fbf37360fb8169a26704c956506ca0deedb8025e3b39.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)$ by $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ units to the right. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{C}{2}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\,⟹\\,C=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\n$$" 13194139535746 true] [17592186364571 1017 #edb/ref 17592186364572 13194139535746 true] [17592186364571 1089 "q-142000" 13194139535746 true] [17592186364571 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535746 true] [17592186364571 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069862/assets/98edb404419e7ab515109f87addb44709513007b1a0105b648a3ce2b62ad547f.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B+D?$" 13194139535746 true] [17592186364571 1113 #uuid "949bd398-6573-53d2-bf2d-21cf0dde3f19" 13194139535746 true] [17592186364571 1145 "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{\\pi }{3})}=3.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $1$ unit up. Hence, $D=1.$\n\nTherefore, $B+D=3+1=4.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x)+1.\n$$" 13194139535746 true] [17592186364572 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364573 13194139535746 true] [17592186364572 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364574 13194139535746 true] [17592186364572 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364575 13194139535746 true] [17592186364572 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364576 13194139535746 true] [17592186364572 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364577 13194139535746 true] [17592186364572 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535746 true] [17592186364572 1041 "selection" 13194139535746 true] [17592186364572 1104 #edb/ref 17592186364576 13194139535746 true] [17592186364572 1110 #uuid "35f9ac41-4f96-571a-8018-1cc0d2bb0983" 13194139535746 true] [17592186364573 1034 "3" 13194139535746 true] [17592186364573 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535746 true] [17592186364573 1111 #uuid "e7ed59c4-7f8d-511d-b2cd-912a8ca1bbd5" 13194139535746 true] [17592186364574 1034 "2" 13194139535746 true] [17592186364574 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535746 true] [17592186364574 1111 #uuid "e36a7df3-4b60-51eb-8410-ea9f59627a4d" 13194139535746 true] [17592186364575 1034 "1" 13194139535746 true] [17592186364575 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535746 true] [17592186364575 1111 #uuid "9a6c8134-625e-517b-b13e-9abb15edb487" 13194139535746 true] [17592186364576 1034 "4" 13194139535746 true] [17592186364576 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535746 true] [17592186364576 1111 #uuid "e58864ec-5f87-5414-bdde-7a3d7aca3efb" 13194139535746 true] [17592186364577 1034 "5" 13194139535746 true] [17592186364577 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535746 true] [17592186364577 1111 #uuid "56d8cf97-4f74-56a0-aa68-2555b3185d47" 13194139535746 true] [17592186364578 1017 #edb/ref 17592186364579 13194139535746 true] [17592186364578 1089 "q-143668" 13194139535746 true] [17592186364578 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535746 true] [17592186364578 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069862/assets/f6ee30d5c189c9579a1f38d59f06581a7cfff944d42489eab2e3c0c6ce089ad4.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B+D?$" 13194139535746 true] [17592186364578 1113 #uuid "e775babe-aff7-50d7-903d-d93ed0c9bdc1" 13194139535746 true] [17592186364578 1145 "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{5\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})}=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $\\frac{12}{5}$ units down. Hence, $D=-\\frac{12}{5}.$\n\nTherefore, $B+D=\\frac{3}{5}+(-\\frac{12}{5})=-\\frac{9}{5}.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{3x}{5})-\\frac{12}{5}.\n$$" 13194139535746 true] [17592186364579 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364580 13194139535746 true] [17592186364579 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364581 13194139535746 true] [17592186364579 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364582 13194139535746 true] [17592186364579 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364583 13194139535746 true] [17592186364579 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364584 13194139535746 true] [17592186364579 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535746 true] [17592186364579 1041 "selection" 13194139535746 true] [17592186364579 1104 #edb/ref 17592186364581 13194139535746 true] [17592186364579 1110 #uuid "a6d8be10-97c4-58fe-8896-2f77d8ac46b3" 13194139535746 true] [17592186364580 1034 "-\\frac{6}{5}" 13194139535746 true] [17592186364580 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535746 true] [17592186364580 1111 #uuid "b94c36f6-5a86-5bea-a203-cacfb66da3ac" 13194139535746 true] [17592186364581 1034 "-\\frac{9}{5}" 13194139535746 true] [17592186364581 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535746 true] [17592186364581 1111 #uuid "0cb3db5c-e6b9-59ca-a168-2117861b71e7" 13194139535746 true] [17592186364582 1034 "3" 13194139535746 true] [17592186364582 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535746 true] [17592186364582 1111 #uuid "1ca5e747-b893-52e8-a886-2ad43b181501" 13194139535746 true] [17592186364583 1034 "\\frac{6}{5}" 13194139535746 true] [17592186364583 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535746 true] [17592186364583 1111 #uuid "eb51bc2d-ca9a-54b1-beaa-611941e8a47c" 13194139535746 true] [17592186364584 1034 "-\\frac{3}{5}" 13194139535746 true] [17592186364584 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535746 true] [17592186364584 1111 #uuid "e29bd491-edc3-5252-a7e1-b44d9ab15add" 13194139535746 true] [17592186364585 1017 #edb/ref 17592186364586 13194139535746 true] [17592186364585 1089 "q-143674" 13194139535746 true] [17592186364585 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535746 true] [17592186364585 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069862/assets/ee4ad78545160a052a28f0f0fcc7df476cc2063e48429a315a9cb9242f7d5311.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x+C),$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of $C?$" 13194139535746 true] [17592186364585 1113 #uuid "fb50bb2b-5c10-590e-b034-9707381ca9b3" 13194139535746 true] [17592186364585 1145 "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3(x+\\frac{C}{3}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{C}{3}$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3(x+\\frac{C}{3}))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069862/assets/e1ed3a322c82b5c70acb01c54c97b09814f41aec80d980034a6fcdc46037209c.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x)$ by $\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\frac{\\pi }{12}=\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ units to the left. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{C}{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\n$$" 13194139535746 true] [17592186364586 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364587 13194139535746 true] [17592186364586 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364588 13194139535746 true] [17592186364586 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364589 13194139535746 true] [17592186364586 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364590 13194139535746 true] [17592186364586 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364591 13194139535746 true] [17592186364586 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535746 true] [17592186364586 1041 "selection" 13194139535746 true] [17592186364586 1104 #edb/ref 17592186364589 13194139535746 true] [17592186364586 1110 #uuid "d4476e94-74ca-573c-b4d8-ca005305943b" 13194139535746 true] [17592186364587 1034 "-\\frac{\\pi }{12}" 13194139535746 true] [17592186364587 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535746 true] [17592186364587 1111 #uuid "1f00686a-47d8-510d-9126-f8a1842eb2bc" 13194139535746 true] [17592186364588 1034 "-\\frac{\\pi }{3}" 13194139535746 true] [17592186364588 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535746 true] [17592186364588 1111 #uuid "ccdc2494-06db-5e3c-8680-e17dc3e997e0" 13194139535746 true] [17592186364589 1034 "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" 13194139535746 true] [17592186364589 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535746 true] [17592186364589 1111 #uuid "9e46b978-291c-5371-8332-f8ac43b31238" 13194139535746 true] [17592186364590 1034 "-\\frac{\\pi }{4}" 13194139535746 true] [17592186364590 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535746 true] [17592186364590 1111 #uuid "39daa270-eb68-5ba9-84a4-cd6aee1b7ef2" 13194139535746 true] [17592186364591 1034 "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" 13194139535746 true] [17592186364591 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535746 true] [17592186364591 1111 #uuid "4e0afeb4-d0c8-524d-ab14-f1b98191d12e" 13194139535746 true] [17592186364592 1017 #edb/ref 17592186364593 13194139535746 true] [17592186364592 1089 "q-65571" 13194139535746 true] [17592186364592 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535746 true] [17592186364592 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069862/assets/2f11d5b11c53f7b826291e0b7e95828f57d6f1242b768baf12c874be229b2935.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3x}{5}+C),$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of $C?$" 13194139535746 true] [17592186364592 1113 #uuid "ad1e1c5f-8601-5df4-a3fc-7ce84f2d264b" 13194139535746 true] [17592186364592 1145 "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3x}{5}+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{5}(x+\\frac{5C}{3}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{5C}{3}$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{5}(x+\\frac{5C}{3}))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3x}{5}).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069862/assets/5e9b6568ec493d95db6fe72a6a19024326c5b920629e9ff44a85db54089aa2a6.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3x}{5})$ by $\\frac{\\pi }{3}-0=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ units to the right. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5C}{3}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\,⟹\\,C=(-\\frac{\\pi }{3})(\\frac{3}{5})\\,⟹\\,C=-\\frac{\\pi }{5}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $-\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ is in this interval, the value of $C$ is $-\\frac{\\pi }{5}.$" 13194139535746 true] [17592186364593 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364594 13194139535746 true] [17592186364593 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364595 13194139535746 true] [17592186364593 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364596 13194139535746 true] [17592186364593 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364597 13194139535746 true] [17592186364593 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364598 13194139535746 true] [17592186364593 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535746 true] [17592186364593 1041 "selection" 13194139535746 true] [17592186364593 1104 #edb/ref 17592186364595 13194139535746 true] [17592186364593 1110 #uuid "fdf59e94-c44c-520b-a6ea-c9da07142fd6" 13194139535746 true] [17592186364594 1034 "\\frac{\\pi }{10}" 13194139535746 true] [17592186364594 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535746 true] [17592186364594 1111 #uuid "4a8a48ad-7fbc-5d7d-b628-66506c916a76" 13194139535746 true] [17592186364595 1034 "-\\frac{\\pi }{5}" 13194139535746 true] [17592186364595 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139